    "display": "160x128 RGB565, LVGL 9.6.0; one 64-row 20,480-byte draw buffer in each implementation",
    "workload": "10 seconds warmup, three ~9.5-second idle windows, then three ~9.5-second forced whole-screen redraw windows. Counters discard transition frames. Frames count completed final LVGL flushes; full-screen pixel accounting is checked. Idle iterations are not FPS.",
    "sampling": "Both sensor producers run at 25 Hz. Rust retains its 100 Hz control task; native C++ retains 50 Hz. All production application tasks retained. Measurement hooks do not inject control inputs or arm the device.",
    "configuration": "Native C++ ESP-IDF 5.5.3 uses -O2. Rust release remains opt-level=s with thin LTO; LVGL/libcanard use static C code. Both use the same configured SPI clocks for each comparison. These are tuned implementation comparisons, not an isolated language benchmark.",
    "clock": "16 and 40 MHz are requested/configured clocks; no external logic-analyzer waveform measurement. Native production default remains 16 MHz; Rust production default remains 40 MHz.",
    "timing": "UI/flush/control values are wall-clock elapsed times and include preemption and synchronous IO. They are not measurements of exclusive CPU utilization. Overrun counters compare control execution duration against the configured period.",
